(version 1)

# Board3 scoped DRC exceptions -- reviewed 2026-07-30 (fix/board3-cosmetic-drc).
# These encode WHY the geometry is acceptable instead of muting findings;
# the global rules stay armed everywhere else.

# U1's decoupling ring sits deliberately tight against the LQFP-144 --
# moving the caps away to satisfy courtyard etiquette would lengthen the
# supply loops and worsen the design. Also covers the buck pair (U3/L2,
# kept tight for commutation loop area) and the R9/R45 QSPI neighbors
# (0.08 mm courtyard kiss).
(rule tight_courtyards_by_design
  (condition "A.Reference == 'U1' || B.Reference == 'U1' || A.Reference == 'R9' || A.Reference == 'R45' || A.Reference == 'U3' || A.Reference == 'L2'")
  (constraint courtyard_clearance (min -0.6mm)))

# U12 (AW9523B telltale expander) sits deliberately close to LED6, the
# telltale it drives -- the east cluster is packed against the board's
# 50 mm height. This overlap was INVISIBLE until 2026-07-31: the
# AW9523BTQR footprint carried no courtyard at all, so every courtyard
# check passed U11/U12 by testing nothing (pre-fab review, CONFIRMED
# HIGH). With the courtyard added (pad extent 4.7 mm + 0.25 mm margin =
# 5.2 mm square, matching the WSON-8's convention) the real adjacency
# finally reports. Measured, not assumed: bodies clear by 0.90 mm, pads
# by 0.20 mm -- both comfortable. Only the courtyards kiss, by 0.30 mm.
(rule telltale_expander_cluster
  (condition "(A.Reference == 'U12' && B.Reference == 'LED6') || (A.Reference == 'LED6' && B.Reference == 'U12')")
  (constraint courtyard_clearance (min -0.4mm)))

# JLC publishes TWO hole-to-track limits and KiCad's min_hole_clearance is a
# single value, so one of them cannot be expressed globally: via-to-track is
# 0.2 mm, PTH-to-track is 0.28 mm (0.35 recommended). The global rule carries
# 0.2 because that is the binding limit for the tightest hole on this board
# (0.2730 mm, a via). This rule adds the stricter PTH case on top.
#
# It catches nothing today -- measured 2026-08-05, the tightest PTH-to-track is
# 0.4547 mm at SW6.2 against /BTN2 on Inner1, and NO pad sits in the 0.20-0.28
# band a global 0.2 rule would wave through. That is the same argument U38/U43
# made when it raised five rails to the fab's real limits: "None of these was a
# present defect. They were checks that COULD NOT catch a defect." A board with
# 43 PTH pads should not rely on the via limit to police them.
(rule pth_hole_to_track_jlc
  (condition "A.Pad_Type == 'Through-hole' || B.Pad_Type == 'Through-hole'")
  (constraint hole_clearance (min 0.28mm)))

# USBC1 is a mid-mount connector: its retention slots are routed cutouts
# inside its own manufacturer footprint, and its pads plus the local
# shield stitching legitimately sit closer to those slots than the
# board-wide copper-to-edge rule allows. Scoped by the usbc_slots rule
# area drawn on the board around the connector.
(rule usbc_slot_edge
  (condition "A.intersectsArea('usbc_slots')")
  (constraint edge_clearance (min 0.2mm)))

# KiCad runs NO silkscreen test unless a silk_clearance rule exists in THIS
# file. Board3's .kicad_pro carries min_silk_clearance 0.15 and has
# silk_over_copper / silk_overlap / silk_edge_clearance all armed at warning,
# and every one of those is inert on its own -- DRC reported 0 at
# --severity-all while JLCPCB's DFM returned 81 red silkscreen errors on the
# same board. Three settings that look configured and check nothing.
#
# Scoped to pads on purpose. Unconditioned, the constraint also measures silk
# against F.Courtyard rectangles and Component Marking Layer fields; neither is
# plotted (kicad_fab.py exports 11 layers and Courtyard is not one), so those
# findings demand work that cannot change the board JLCPCB builds. Measured
# 2026-08-05: unconditioned 18 + 199, scoped to pads 9 + 0.
#
# Verified to FIRE, not merely to pass: at 0.60mm this same rule reports 199 of
# each. A rule that reports 0 because it is malformed and one that reports 0
# because the board is clean are indistinguishable from the passing side.
(rule jlc_silk_to_pad
  (condition "B.Type == 'Pad'")
  (constraint silk_clearance (min 0.15mm)))

# Two documented cases that cannot reach 0.15 mm, held to their measured worst
# rather than muted. Both are clipped by --subtract-soldermask in the plotted
# gerber, so nothing prints on a pad either way; what is being accepted is a
# marking slightly shorter than drawn.
#
#   D10/D11  the 1N5819WS cathode bands, 0.1000 mm from their OWN cathode pad.
#            A polarity marker is the one silk feature whose meaning dies if it
#            is trimmed, so it is kept whole.
#   R9       an R0603 whose body outline sits 0.1051 mm from U1.52. R9 is 1 of
#            31 R0603 placements sharing one library definition, and this board
#            deliberately packs it against the LQFP-144 (see the R9/R45 QSPI
#            courtyard allowance above). Trimming this instance alone would
#            make the type divergent and unmirrorable for a marking that is
#            unreadable in that gap anyway.
(rule silk_to_pad_documented_exceptions
  (condition "B.Type == 'Pad' && (A.Reference == 'D10' || A.Reference == 'D11' || A.Reference == 'R9')")
  (constraint silk_clearance (min 0.09mm)))
